# Multi-task LSTM for Population-Relevant Air Pollution Prediction

This repository provides the implementation of a distributed multi-task
Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model for predicting six major air pollutants
(PM2.5, PM10, CO, NO2, SO2, and O3) using meteorological and reanalysis inputs.

The framework is designed for large-scale spatiotemporal datasets and
supports distributed training with PyTorch Distributed Data Parallel (DDP).

## Key Features
- Multi-task learning with a shared LSTM backbone
- Pollutant-specific task heads
- Distributed training on multi-GPU systems
- Chunk-wise data loading for memory efficiency
- Compatible with population-weighted exposure assessment

## Project Structure
```text
src/
  models/        Neural network architectures
  data/          Data loading and preprocessing
  train/         Training and evaluation logic
  utils/         Utilities (DDP, logging, plotting)

Data Description

Meteorological inputs are stored as hourly .npy files.
Ground-based air pollution observations are provided as station-level CSV files.
Each grid cell is matched to the nearest monitoring station.


Training

torchrun --nproc_per_node=4 src/main.py

Output

Trained multi-task model weights (.pth)

Training loss curves for overall and task-specific losses

Evaluation metrics (R² and RMSE) for each pollutant

Reproducibility

Random seeds are fixed where applicable.
All preprocessing scalers are loaded from serialized joblib objects.

